  "data": {
    "text": "Modular segregation of task-dependent brain networks contributes to the development of executive function in children\n\nNeuroImage 206 (2020) 116334\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2019.116334\n1053-8119/© 2019 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Inc. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license.\n\nEighty-eight children, ranging in age from 7 to 12, were recruited from a single district within Qiqihar city, Heilongjiang Province. This study was approved by the research ethics review board of Zhejiang University in China, and was conducted in accordance with the guidelines of Helsinki Declaration. Informed consent was obtained from both the children and their parents. All participants were from urban families. They had normal hearing, normal or corrected-to-normal vision, no history of neurological or psychiatric disorder, and no reports of special educational assistance requirements. Six children were excluded because of behavioral data missing; fifteen children were excluded because of excessive head motion during fMRI scanning (exceeding 3.0mm in displacement or 3° in rotation), yielding sixty-seven children in the final sample (38 boys, mean age = 10.03 ± 1.57, range = 7.18–12.48).\n\nThe Dots task adapted from Davidson et al. (2006) was used to measure EF in this study. In this task, two different task conditions (single-task and mixed-task) were designed to probe EF ability. The single-task condition included two types of blocks (single-congruent and single-incongruent). The single-congruent blocks presented only stripped dots, and subjects were instructed to respond on the same side as the stripped dots. The single-incongruent blocks presented only gray dots, and subjects were instructed to respond on the opposite side to the gray dots. The mixed-task condition randomly presented stripped or gray dots, and subjects were instructed to make the side-congruent or side-incongruent responses according to which types of dots were presented.\n\nA block design was used in the fMRI experiment with two sessions of scanning. During each session, there were three blocks presenting only stripped dots (single-task), three blocks presenting only gray dots (single-task), and three blocks randomly presenting stripped and gray dots (mixed-task). Each block had a 26-s task condition, followed by a 12-s fixation slide. The 26-s task condition included a 2-s task instruction and twelve 2-s trials. In each trial, a fixation was presented on the screen for 500ms; subsequently, a stripped or gray dot was displayed on the left or right side of the fixation for 750ms or disappeared if a response was given; finally, a blank interval was presented for a variable duration in order to keep each trial last for 2 s. There was also a 10-s pre-scan performed before each task session to obtain a stable signal. Thus, the scanning duration for the whole task-state was 704 s, resulting in 352 functional volumes.\n\nImaging was performed on a 1.5 T Philips MRI scanner with a standard eight-channel head coil. For each participant, a high-resolution brain structural image was obtained using a 3-dimensional fast field echo (FFE) sequence (TR = 25ms, TE = 4.6ms, flip angle = 15°, 150 slices in the sagittal plane, FOV = 256 × 256 mm², matrix size = 256 × 256). Functional images were acquired using a single-shot echo-planar imaging (EPI) sequence (TR = 2000ms, TE = 50ms, flip angle = 90°, 22 interleaved ascending slices per volume, slice thickness = 5mm, slice gap = 0.8mm, FOV = 230 × 230 mm², matrix size = 64 × 64).\n\nThe imaging data were preprocessed using the Data Processing Assistant for Resting-state fMRI Advanced Edition (DPARSF-A, version 4.3) toolbox. Preprocessing steps included discarding the five pre-scans, slice-timing correction, and head-motion correction. Then the functional images were aligned to the corresponding T1-weighted images, and were normalized to the MNI space with a resampling voxel size of 3 × 3 × 3 mm³. Finally, the functional images were smoothed using a 6mm full-width half-maximum (FWHM) Gaussian kernel. Frame-wise displacement (FD) was used to characterize head motion inside the scanner. A censoring/scrubbing strategy was used to remove time points with FD greater than 0.3mm. Task-dependent functional connectivity was assessed using the CONN toolbox. For each participant, the five components of white-matter and cerebrospinal fluid signals, the six motion parameters and their first-order derivatives, and global signal time courses were extracted and regressed out from the data. Task-induced neural effects were removed using separate block regressors convolved with a canonical hemodynamic response function and their first-derivative terms. A high pass filter with a cut-off frequency of 0.01 Hz was also implemented for the imaging data.\n\nTo obtain defined nodes, we applied a functional template proposed by Dosenbach et al. (2010) that parcellates the brain into 160 functionally segregated regions of interest (ROIs) covering most of the cerebral cortex and cerebellum. Average time courses from each ROI were extracted and pair-wise Pearson correlation coefficients were computed between these ROIs. We used a proportional threshold (sparsity) of 15% to ensure the same number of network edges for each participant. According to Dosenbach et al. (2010), the 160 ROIs were assigned into six modules: the default-mode network (DMN), frontal–parietal network (FPN), cingulo-opercular network (CONN), sensorimotor network (SMN), visual network, and the cerebellum. All graph theory measures were calculated using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox. Participation coefficient (PC) was calculated, and we also computed the number of connections within each module and between modules.\n\nPartial correlation analyses were used to examine the associations between age, task performance and brain network properties, with gender, intelligence and head motion as covariates, and Bonferroni correction applied. Behavioral performance measures included accuracy (ACC) and mean response time (RT). We also performed an exploratory mediation analysis using the PROCESS macro for SPSS 22.0 with 10,000 bootstrapped samples."
  },
